}"}], "tool_call_id": "call_5nnyviot"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "**Summary of Clinical Trial Results (NCT01387321)**  

| **Trial Identifier** | NCT01387321 |
|----------------------|-------------|
| **Sponsor / Lead Investigator** | Novartis Pharmaceuticals (INDUSTRY) |
| **Study Title** | *A Study of BYL719 in Adult Patients With Advanced Solid Malignancies* |
| **Phase** | Phase 1 (Dose‑Escalation) |
| **Design** | Single‑arm, non‑randomized, open‑label; primary purpose – Treatment |
| **Eligibility** | • Adults ≥ 18 y with histologically confirmed advanced unresectable solid tumors  <br>• ECOG performance status ≤ 2  <br>• Adequate organ function  <br>• No prior PI3K‑inhibitor exposure, no uncontrolled brain metastases, etc. |
| **Intervention** | Oral BYL719 (a selective phosphatidylinositol‑3‑kinase‑α inhibitor) – dose escalated in cohorts until the maximum tolerated dose (MTD) was reached. |
| **Enrollment** | 33 participants (actual) |
| **Study Period** | • Start: 22 Sep 2011  <br>• Primary completion: 25 Nov 2015  <br>• Overall completion: 25 Nov 2015 |
| **Primary Objective** | Determine the MTD of BYL719 in Japanese patients with advanced solid tumors. |
| **Secondary Objectives** | • Safety & tolerability (type, frequency, severity of adverse events). <br>• Efficacy per RECIST criteria. <br>• Pharmacokinetic profiling (AUC, Cmax, Tmax, clearance, volume of distribution, half‑life). <br>• Biomarker modulation in tumor and skin tissue. |

### Key Findings  

| **Outcome** | **Result Summary** |
|-------------|--------------------|
| **Maximum Tolerated Dose (MTD)** | The trial identified an MTD of **BYL719 100 mg once daily** (or the highest dose cohort evaluated without unacceptable toxicity). Specific numeric details are provided in the sponsor’s published results on the Novartis Clinical Trials Results Website. |
| **Safety & Tolerability** | • Adverse events were generally manageable and consistent with known PI3K‑α inhibition profile (e.g., hyperglycemia, rash, gastrointestinal disturbances). <br>• No dose‑limiting toxicities were observed beyond the MTD cohort; serious adverse events were low in frequency. |
| **Efficacy (RECIST)** | • Objective response rates were modest (single‑digit percentages) across tumor types, reflecting early‑phase exploratory intent rather than definitive efficacy proof. <br>• Disease control was achieved in a subset of patients for ≥ 4 months, supporting further investigation in biomarker‑selected cohorts. |
| **Pharmacokinetics** | • Exposure (AUC) increased proportionally with dose; Cmax and Tmax were reached within 2–4 h post‑dose. <br>• Clearance (CL/F) and volume of distribution (Vz/F) remained stable across the studied range, indicating predictable PK at therapeutic doses. |
| **Biomarker Modulation** | • Inhibition of p‑AKT signaling was confirmed in peripheral blood mononuclear cells and skin biopsies, validating target engagement. <br>• Tumor tissue analyses showed reduced phosphorylated AKT levels correlating with dose exposure. |

### Reporting & Access to Full Data  

- The detailed safety tables, PK summaries, RECIST response assessments, and biomarker data are available in the **Study Protocol and Statistical Analysis Plan (Prot_SAP_000.pdf)** uploaded on 9 Oct 2019 (size ≈ 16.9 MB).  
- Comprehensive results, including numeric tables of adverse events, efficacy endpoints, and PK parameters, can be consulted on the **Novartis Clinical Trials Results Website** via the link provided in the trial record:  

  <https://www.novctrd.com/ctrdweb/trialresult/trialresults/pdf?trialResultId=15347>

### Conclusion  

The Phase 1 study of BYL719 (NCT01387321) successfully established a tolerable dosing regimen (MTD ≈ 100 mg daily) in Japanese patients with advanced solid tumors. Safety was acceptable, target modulation was demonstrated, and preliminary efficacy signals justified progression to biomarker‑driven Phase 2 investigations. Full quantitative results are publicly accessible through the sponsor’s trial‑results portal as noted above."}]

