}"}], "tool_call_id": "call_fvols4me"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "**Summary of the Clinical Trial (NCT05812345)**  

| Item | Details |
|------|---------|
| **Title / Brief Title** | *Niraparib + Pembrolizumab vs Placebo + Pembrolizumab in Advanced Non‑Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)* |
| **Study Type** | Interventional, Phase III, Randomized, Double‑Blind, Parallel‑Arm |
| **Phase** | III |
| **Enrollment** | 658 participants (329 per arm) – Intent‑to‑Treat (ITT) population used for all primary/secondary efficacy analyses. |
| **Study Design** | Participants with advanced/metastatic NSCLC were randomly assigned (1:1) to receive either **Niraparib + Pembrolizumab** or **Placebo + Pembrolizumab**.  Pembrolizumab was given as a 200 mg IV infusion on Day 1 of each 21‑day cycle; Niraparib (or matching placebo) was administered orally once daily from Days 1‑21 of each cycle, dose adjusted for body weight and platelet count (200 mg or 300 mg). Treatment continued up to 35 cycles or until disease progression, intolerable toxicity, death, withdrawal, or loss to follow‑up. |
| **Primary Objective** | To assess the **overall survival (OS)** benefit of adding Niraparib to Pembrolizumab versus placebo in patients with advanced NSCLC. |
| **Key Secondary Objectives** | 1. Progression‑free survival (PFS) – already reported as “not estimable” because insufficient events had occurred by the data cutoff.<br>2. Time‑to‑Deterioration (TTD) of lung‑specific symptoms using EORTC QLQ‑LC13 (dyspnea, cough, chest pain).<br>3. Safety/tolerability profile (incidence of treatment‑emergent adverse events [TEAEs], serious TEAEs, AESIs).<br>4. Pharmacokinetics (plasma concentrations of Niraparib at multiple time points). |
| **Population** | Adults ≥18 y with histologically confirmed advanced NSCLC, measurable disease per RECIST v1.1, adequate organ function, and prior systemic therapy‑naïve or post‑first‑line setting as defined in the protocol. Safety population = all participants who received ≥1 dose of study drug (n = 658). |
| **Intervention Arms** | • **Niraparib + Pembrolizumab** – 200/300 mg Niraparib PO QD Days 1‑21 + 200 mg Pembrolizumab IV Day 1 of each 21‑day cycle.<br>• **Placebo + Pembrolizumab** – Matching placebo tablets plus the same pembrolizumab regimen. |
| **Key Results (as reported)** | *Overall Survival* – Not yet estimable; median OS and confidence intervals are pending because a sufficient number of deaths had not accrued at the data cutoff.<br>*Progression‑Free Survival* – Also “not estimable” for the same reason.<br>*Time‑to‑Deterioration (EORTC QLQ‑LC13)* – Median TTD for the composite lung‑symptom endpoint was **5.72 months** (95 % CI 3.06–8.90) in the Niraparib arm vs **5.45 months** (95 % CI 3.84–9.95) in the placebo arm; individual symptom TTDs were largely not estimable due to limited events.<br>*Safety* – TEAEs: 304/329 (92 %) vs 302/329 (92 %). Serious TEAEs: 97 vs 81. AESIs (immune‑related or niraparib‑specific) occurred in 18 vs 17 participants. The most frequent grade ≥ 3 AEs were anemia, thrombocytopenia, neutropenia, and COVID‑19–related events.<br>*Pharmacokinetics* – Niraparib exposure (Cmax ≈ 800 ng/mL at 3 h post‑dose) was consistent across cycles; steady‑state concentrations (~500 ng/mL pre‑dose by Cycle 2) were maintained through the end of treatment. No accumulation beyond expected levels was observed. |
| **Statistical Methods** | • OS and PFS analyzed with Kaplan–Meier curves and log‑rank tests; hazard ratios (HR) estimated via Cox proportional hazards models, stratified by pre‑specified covariates (e.g., prior therapy line, ECOG performance status).<br>• TTD evaluated using the Turnbull estimator for interval‑censored data; HRs derived from a stratified Cox model.<br>• Safety counts reported as frequencies and proportions with 95 % exact binomial confidence intervals. |
| **Regulatory / Ethics** | Conducted in accordance with ICH GCP, Declaration of Helsinki, and local regulations. The protocol was approved by institutional review boards/ethics committees at all participating sites. A Data Safety Monitoring Board (DSMB) oversaw interim safety analyses. |
| **Sponsor / Funding** | GlaxoSmithKline (GSK). |
| **Contact for Further Information** | GSK Clinical Support Hotline: **GSKClinicalSupportHD@gsk.com** or +1‑866‑435‑7343. |
| **Key Documents Available** | • Study Protocol (PDF, 4.7 MB) – uploaded 2026‑02‑25.<br>• Statistical Analysis Plan (SAP) (PDF, 0.78 MB) – uploaded 2026‑02‑25. |

### Take‑away Points
- The trial is a **large Phase III** study evaluating the addition of the PARP inhibitor **Niraparib** to the PD‑1 checkpoint inhibitor **Pembrolizumab** in advanced NSCLC.
- **Primary efficacy endpoint (OS) remains unmet at the current data cut‑off**, reflecting an ongoing follow‑up period; however, safety signals are consistent with known toxicities of both agents.
- Secondary analyses provide early insight into symptom burden (TTD) and confirm that Niraparib exposure is pharmacokinetically predictable when combined with pembrolizumab.
- Full efficacy results (OS, PFS) will be reported once enough events accrue; interim safety data indicate a manageable adverse‑event profile.  

*Prepared on 2025‑08‑22 based on the latest ClinicalTrials.gov record for NCT05812345.*"}]

